    "\n",
    "# Statistical Learning Basics\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "### What Is Statistical Learning?\n",
    "\n",
    "Statistical learning studies how to use data to understand relationships and make predictions.\n",
    "\n",
    "A common abstract model is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Y = f(X) + \\varepsilon,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $X$ represents predictors;\n",
    "- $Y$ represents the response;\n",
    "- $f$ is the underlying relationship;\n",
    "- $\\varepsilon$ represents random noise or unexplained variation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main objective of statistical learning is to estimate the unknown function $f$ using observed data.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Core Perspective\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern statistical learning is fundamentally about:\n",
    "\n",
    "> learning patterns that generalize well to unseen data.\n",
    "\n",
    "This perspective motivates many major topics:\n",
    "\n",
    "- linear models,\n",
    "- regularization,\n",
    "- trees,\n",
    "- boosting,\n",
    "- neural networks,\n",
    "- ensemble methods,\n",
    "- cross-validation.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Classical Statistics vs Modern Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "Historically:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Classical Statistics | Modern Machine Learning |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| inference | prediction |\n",
    "| interpretable models | predictive performance |\n",
    "| smaller datasets | large datasets |\n",
    "| stronger assumptions | weaker assumptions |\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern statistical learning often combines ideas from both areas.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Types of Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Supervised Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "In supervised learning, we observe both predictors $X$ and response $Y$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to predict or explain the response.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- house price prediction,\n",
    "- spam detection,\n",
    "- medical diagnosis.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Regression and Classification\n",
    "\n",
    "Within supervised learning, two major tasks are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **regression**, where the response is quantitative;\n",
    "- **classification**, where the response is categorical.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- predicting salary is a regression problem;\n",
    "- predicting whether a tumor is benign or malignant is a classification problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Unsupervised Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "In unsupervised learning, we observe predictors $X$ but no response $Y$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to discover hidden structure in the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- clustering,\n",
    "- dimensionality reduction,\n",
    "- anomaly detection.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Reinforcement Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "In reinforcement learning, an agent interacts with an environment and learns through rewards and penalties.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- robotics,\n",
    "- game playing,\n",
    "- recommendation systems.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Different Types of Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Machine learning methods are strongly connected to data structure, and different data types often require different modeling strategies.\n",
    "\n",
    "This course mainly focuses on **tabular data**, which is the classical setting in statistics and applied machine learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Tabular Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Tabular data is typically represented as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times p},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $n$ is the number of observations;\n",
    "- $p$ is the number of predictors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Age | Income | Balance | Student | Default |\n",
    "|---:|---:|---:|:---:|:---:|\n",
    "| 23 | 50000 | 1200 | Yes | No |\n",
    "| 45 | 90000 | 3000 | No | Yes |\n",
    "\n",
    "In tabular data:\n",
    "\n",
    "- rows represent observations;\n",
    "- columns represent variables or features.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- finance,\n",
    "- healthcare,\n",
    "- insurance,\n",
    "- marketing,\n",
    "- scientific experiments.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "## Main Idea\n",
    "\n",
    "The key feature of tabular data is not merely the matrix representation. More importantly, each column corresponds to a variable with its own semantic meaning, such as age, income, blood pressure, or transaction count.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
    "## Other types: Image Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Image data contains spatial structure, meaning nearby pixels are highly related.\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern image models frequently use:\n",
    "\n",
    "- convolutional neural networks,\n",
    "- vision transformers.\n",
    "\n",
    "A central goal is to extract meaningful visual patterns such as edges, textures, shapes, and objects.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
    "## Other types: Text Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Text data contains sequential and contextual structure.\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern NLP is largely based on:\n",
    "\n",
    "- transformers,\n",
    "- large language models.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
    "## Other types: Time Series Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Time series data is ordered over time.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- stock prices,\n",
    "- weather data,\n",
    "- sensor measurements.\n",
    "\n",
    "Important ideas include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- trend,\n",
    "- seasonality,\n",
    "- temporal dependence.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
    "## Other types: Graph Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Graph data represents relationships between objects.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- social networks,\n",
    "- citation networks,\n",
    "- molecular structures.\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern graph learning often uses graph neural networks.\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Main Questions in Statistical Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "At a high level, statistical learning studies one central question:\n",
    "\n",
    "> How can we learn useful patterns from data?\n",
    "\n",
    "This leads to several different kinds of questions.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "Prediction asks:\n",
    "\n",
    "> What will happen for a new observation?\n",
    "\n",
    "Prediction is often the primary goal in modern machine learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "Mathematically, prediction is often viewed as estimating\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat f(x) \\approx \\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid X=x].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Will a customer default?\n",
    "- What will tomorrow's demand be?\n",
    "- Will a patient develop a disease?\n",
    "\n",
    "In practice, prediction mainly focuses on predictive performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Inference and Understanding\n",
    "\n",
    "Inference asks:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Which variables matter, and how are they related to the response?\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Does smoking increase disease risk?\n",
    "- Which features drive customer churn?\n",
    "- Which predictors are statistically significant?\n",
    "\n",
    "Common tools include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- standard errors,\n",
    "- confidence intervals,\n",
    "- hypothesis testing.\n",
    "\n",
    "Inference usually relies more heavily on model assumptions and diagnostics.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Causality\n",
    "\n",
    "Causality asks:\n",
    "\n",
    "> What happens if we intervene?\n",
    "\n",
    "A causal question is often written as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid do(X=x)].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- randomized experiments,\n",
    "- instrumental variables,\n",
    "- difference-in-differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "## Important Distinctions\n",
    "\n",
    "| Task | Main Question |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Prediction | What will happen? |\n",
    "| Inference | Is there a relationship? |\n",
    "| Causality | What happens if we intervene? |\n",
    "\n",
    "Important points:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Good training performance does not imply good generalization.\n",
    "2. Good prediction does not imply valid inference.\n",
    "3. Association does not imply causation.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Generalization and Model Complexity\n",
    "\n",
    "### Training Error and Test Error\n",
    "\n",
    "Classical regression focuses heavily on checking model assumptions, such as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- independence of errors,\n",
    "- constant variance,\n",
    "- approximate normality.\n",
    "\n",
    "These assumptions are especially important for formal inference, including confidence intervals and hypothesis testing.\n",
    "\n",
    "In statistical learning, however, the primary goal is often **predictive performance** rather than formal inference. As a result, diagnostic tools still remain useful, but they usually play a secondary role. Their main purpose is often to detect serious issues such as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- strong nonlinearity,\n",
    "- influential observations,\n",
    "- data quality problems,\n",
    "- severe model misspecification.\n",
    "\n",
    "The central question becomes:\n",
    "\n",
    "> How well does the model predict new observations?\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {#def-training-error}\n",
    "## Training Error\n",
    "\n",
    "The training error measures how well a model fits the training data.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {#def-test-error}\n",
    "## Test Error\n",
    "\n",
    "The test error measures prediction error on new observations not used to fit the model.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, suppose we evaluate a regression model using mean squared error (MSE).\n",
    "\n",
    "The **training MSE** measures fit on the observed training sample, while the **test MSE** measures prediction performance on new data drawn from the same population.\n",
    "\n",
    "A model may achieve extremely small training error while still performing poorly on unseen data. This happens because the model begins fitting random noise rather than the underlying signal.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {#def-overfitting}\n",
    "## Overfitting\n",
    "\n",
    "Overfitting occurs when a model fits the training data very closely but fails to generalize well to new data. In this case, the model captures random noise or idiosyncrasies in the training sample rather than the underlying relationship.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal of statistical learning is therefore:\n",
    "\n",
    "> to build models with small test error, not merely small training error.\n",
    "\n",
    "Model selection methods attempt to balance model complexity and prediction accuracy in order to avoid overfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Bias--Variance Tradeoff\n",
    "\n",
    "A central idea behind generalization is the **bias--variance tradeoff**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Y=f(X)+\\varepsilon,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\Var(\\varepsilon)=\\sigma^2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For a fitted estimator $\\hat f$, the test mean squared error at $x_0$ satisfies\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\mathbb{E}\n",
    "\\left[\n",
    "(Y_0-\\hat f(x_0))^2\n",
    "\\right]\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\operatorname{Bias}^2(\\hat f(x_0))\n",
    "+\n",
    "\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat f(x_0))\n",
    "+\n",
    "\\sigma^2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The three terms represent:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **bias**: systematic modeling error;\n",
    "- **variance**: sensitivity to the training sample;\n",
    "- **irreducible error**: noise that cannot be removed.\n",
    "\n",
    "Roughly speaking:\n",
    "\n",
    "- simple models:\n",
    "  - higher bias,\n",
    "  - lower variance;\n",
    "- flexible models:\n",
    "  - lower bias,\n",
    "  - higher variance.\n",
    "\n",
    "As model flexibility increases:\n",
    "\n",
    "- training error usually decreases;\n",
    "- test error often first decreases and then increases.\n",
    "\n",
    "Model selection aims to balance these two effects in order to achieve good generalization performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "## Main Point\n",
    "\n",
    "Many modern methods can be viewed as ways to control the bias--variance tradeoff. Regularization, cross-validation, ensemble methods, trees, boosting, and neural networks can all be understood from this perspective.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
    "## Information Criteria and Estimated Test Error\n",
    "\n",
    "Classical regression often estimates expected test error indirectly using only the training data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Some commonly used criteria are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- AIC (Akaike Information Criterion),\n",
    "- BIC (Bayesian Information Criterion),\n",
    "- Mallows' $C_p$.\n",
    "\n",
    "These criteria reward good fit while penalizing excessive model complexity.\n",
    "\n",
    "Although they are useful for model comparison, they still rely on approximations derived from the training sample.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## A Typical Statistical Learning Workflow\n",
    "\n",
    "A practical workflow often looks like this:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. define the problem;\n",
    "2. preprocess and explore the data;\n",
    "3. choose candidate models;\n",
    "4. estimate test error using validation or cross-validation;\n",
    "5. compare models;\n",
    "6. interpret and communicate results.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "The overall goal is:\n",
    "\n",
    "> to build models that generalize well while remaining scientifically useful. "
